' Concentration, centralisation, and the reserve army: class model
' Companion notes: notes/concentration.md — full stereotype legend: notes/stereotypes.md
'
' The rest of Capital I, ch. 25: accumulation enlarges individual capitals
' (concentration); competition and credit fuse many into few (centralisation);
' the demand for labour-power lags the growth of capital; a reserve army
' is necessary to the relation, not a policy accident (Marx; SPGB).

@startuml concentration
title VIII. Concentration, centralisation, and the reserve army
caption The general law of capitalist accumulation: notes/concentration.md

hide circle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam backgroundColor white
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam ranksep 50
skinparam nodesep 35
skinparam class {
  BackgroundColor<<social relation>> #EDE7F6
  BackgroundColor<<historically specific>> #FFEBEE
  BackgroundColor<<personification>> #FFF8E1
  BackgroundColor<<abstract det.>> #E3F2FD
}

package "I. Accumulation continues" as P1 {
  class AccumulationOfCapital <<historically specific>> {
    capitalisation of surplus value
    (full definition: accumulation.puml)
  }
  class CapitalistCompetition <<social relation>> <<historically specific>> {
    many capitals
    (full definition: accumulation.puml)
  }
  class Credit <<historically specific>> {
    lever of centralisation
    (banking: finance.puml)
  }
}

package "II. Fewer, larger capitals" as P2 {
  class ConcentrationOfCapital <<historically specific>> {
    individual capitals grow
    by their own accumulation
  }
  class CentralisationOfCapital <<historically specific>> {
    already-existing capitals fused
    expropriation of capitalist
    by capitalist
  }
  class BarrierToEntry <<historically specific>> {
    minimum capital to compete
    rises with scale
  }
}

package "III. Composition and the demand for labour" as P3 {
  class OrganicComposition <<social relation>> <<historically specific>> {
    c / v
    tends to rise with accumulation
    (also in prices-of-production.puml)
  }
  class DemandForLabourPower <<historically specific>> {
    grows more slowly than
    total capital
  }
}

package "IV. The reserve army" as P4 {
  class WageLabourer <<personification>> {
    employed or disposable
    (full definition: surplus-value-capital.puml)
  }
  class ReserveArmyOfLabour <<historically specific>> <<social relation>> {
    surplus population relative
    to capital's need for v
    expands and contracts with the cycle
  }
  class RelativeSurplusPopulation <<historically specific>> {
    floating, latent, stagnant
    produced by accumulation itself
  }
}

class UnemploymentAsPolicyFailure <<abstract det.>> {
  {abstract}
  rejected claim: joblessness is
  a correctable mistake of government
  or an absolute over-population
}

AccumulationOfCapital --> ConcentrationOfCapital : enlarges individual capitals as
CapitalistCompetition --> CentralisationOfCapital : fuses capitals as
Credit --> CentralisationOfCapital : accelerates
ConcentrationOfCapital --> BarrierToEntry : raises
CentralisationOfCapital --> BarrierToEntry : raises
AccumulationOfCapital --> OrganicComposition : tends to raise
OrganicComposition --> DemandForLabourPower : limits
ConcentrationOfCapital --> DemandForLabourPower : fewer workers per capital
DemandForLabourPower --> ReserveArmyOfLabour : leaves a surplus as
RelativeSurplusPopulation --|> ReserveArmyOfLabour
ReserveArmyOfLabour o-- WageLabourer : includes the disposable
ReserveArmyOfLabour --> WageLabourer : disciplines wages of
UnemploymentAsPolicyFailure ..> ReserveArmyOfLabour : mistakes a necessary form for
AccumulationOfCapital ..> UnemploymentAsPolicyFailure : produces the surplus population\n(not a policy error)
CapitalistCompetition ..> UnemploymentAsPolicyFailure : cannot abolish while\nmany capitals accumulate
BarrierToEntry ..> CapitalistCompetition : restricts who can enter

P1 -[hidden]right-> P2
P2 -[hidden]down-> P3
P3 -[hidden]right-> P4

note as NLaw
  **General law (Capital I, ch. 25)**
  The same accumulation that piles up
  wealth as capital produces, relatively,
  a surplus working population.
  Not Malthus: not too many people
  for nature — too many for capital's
  demand for labour-power.
end note

note bottom of CentralisationOfCapital
  Concentration needs growth of social
  capital. Centralisation does not:
  it redistributes existing capitals.
  Credit is a weapon in that war.
end note

note right of ReserveArmyOfLabour
  Necessary to sudden expansion
  and to holding wages within
  limits compatible with
  valorization. "Full employment"
  as a lasting state is another
  simple-reproduction-style baseline
  that accumulation rules out.
end note

@enduml
